WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:20.979
One tile in this wall is broken. It is already ruined, and nothing you do to it can make it
worse. The eight sound tiles around it are the job, and every decision from here is about them.

2
00:00:21.479 --> 00:00:37.269
Safety goggles, because a struck tile does not crumble, it splinters, and it splinters upwards.
Thick gloves, because a freshly broken glazed edge cuts like glass, which is very nearly what it is.

3
00:00:37.769 --> 00:00:51.608
Two strips of masking tape across the diagonals, forming an X. The tape stops the
drill slipping on the glaze, and later it carries most of the broken tile away with it.

4
00:00:51.828 --> 00:01:12.726
Now drill holes along both strips with a five millimetre masonry bit. Only as deep as the tile. Wrap a piece of tape around
the bit as a depth stop, because behind the tile is wall, and in a kitchen or a bathroom there is often a pipe in it.

5
00:01:13.226 --> 00:01:31.059
Before a single blow, rake the grout out of all four joints. Set grout ties the tile to the tiles beside
it, and while that rim is there a crack has somewhere to travel. Take it out and the tile becomes an island.

6
00:01:31.559 --> 00:01:46.001
The cold chisel starts in the middle, where the drilling has already weakened it, and works
outwards to the tape. Start at an edge and you are working against the neighbour, not the wall.

7
00:01:46.221 --> 00:02:05.633
Strike the chisel. Never lever it, because the only thing to lever against is the tile next door, and a tile is
weak in bending. Peel the tape, lift the last pieces with the wood chisel, and the old adhesive is what is left.

8
00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:24.245
Take the old adhesive back with the wood chisel held bevel side down, so the tool rides up out of the work rather
than digging in behind the tile beside it. What you want is flat. Nothing after this sits right on a bed that is not.

9
00:02:24.745 --> 00:02:40.999
Offer the new tile up before any adhesive is opened. If it rocks, that is the wall talking and not the
tile: there is a high spot still on the bed. Go back to the chisel now, while it costs you a minute.

10
00:02:41.499 --> 00:02:56.174
Spread adhesive on the back of the tile with the notched spreader, and use it sparingly. Too much and
the tile stands proud of its neighbours, and you will feel that step every time you wipe the wall.

11
00:02:56.394 --> 00:03:14.320
Press it home until every edge and corner is flush, clean the adhesive off the face, then put the spacers in and
leave it. Twenty four hours, or whatever the container says. The adhesive holds the tile; nothing else does yet.

12
00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:32.931
When it has cured, take the spacers out and grout the four joints, then run the plastic tool along them for
an even line. Polish it off with a dry cloth, and if the job is right nobody can tell which tile it was.

13
00:03:33.431 --> 00:03:57.116
So: protect yourself and the tiles around it. Release it by raking all four joints. Remove it from the centre outwards, striking
and never levering. Replace it on a flat bed, sparingly, flush, and then wait. The repair you cannot find is the finished one.
